  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nExpansive, anomalously strong (approaching 2 sigma) mid level high\ncentered south of the region will predominate through at least\nThursday. This will result in well above normal temperatures and\nheat indices around to slightly above 100. Went ahead and extended\nthe heat advisory out through Thursday. \n\nUpper low tracking across Canada will dig into the Maritimes late in\nthe week which will eventually result in troughing most of the way \ndown the Atlantic coast. This will cause the high to change \norientation and then retrograde over the weekend. While well \nadvertised, this process is trending a bit slower. So, Friday \ntemperatures may not drop quite as much as earlier forecast which may\nresult in heat advisory conditions extending even another day, at\nleast across southern counties. \n\nThe heat wave will ease over the weekend and temperatures will return\ncloser to normal early next week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nWest-east oriented surface front extending across the lower Great \nLakes on Thursday will be a focus for storms to develop as a short\nwave moves over the top of the ridge. While better forcing will be\nnorth of the area, there is some potential for the convection to\nbuild further south into the forecast area late in the day into the\nevening. If this were to occur, then there will the risk of strong to\nsevere storms given sufficient instability and somewhat better shear\nwith the passage of the short wave, especially across the northeast\npart of the forecast area.\n\nAs this front slowly sags south on Friday, the potential for \nconvection will increase across the area. More short wave energy will\nbe moving across the region as the mid level flow becomes\nnorthwesterly in response to the troughing to the northeast. This\nlooks like another day with plenty of instability and reasonable\nshear which could lead to stronger storms.",
